From 9088e6398d5a221d7c6df182f51e2a174c80e344 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 19:42:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][dvbridge] A 1D LUT of the display: a .cube of
 LUT_1D_SIZE, Resolve's shaper and ArgyllCMS's .cal

The display LUT took 3D LUTs only: a .cube with LUT_1D_SIZE was refused,
and the curves that a calibration loads into a video card's gamma table
(ArgyllCMS's .cal, as dispcal and DisplayCAL write it, the "videoLUT")
could not be given at all. A display calibrated with curves alone, or
a .cube made as Resolve makes one with a shaper before its 3D table,
had no way in.

The reader now takes:
- a .cube of LUT_1D_SIZE (2 to 65536) alone: three curves;
- a .cube with LUT_1D_SIZE and LUT_3D_SIZE: Resolve's form, the 1D
  values first, applied as a shaper before the 3D table;
  LUT_1D_INPUT_RANGE must be 0 1, as LUT_3D_INPUT_RANGE;
- ArgyllCMS's .cal (CGATS): the identifier CAL, DEVICE_CLASS "DISPLAY",
  COLOR_REP "RGB", the fields RGB_I, RGB_R, RGB_G and RGB_B in any
  order, RGB_I on a uniform grid from 0 to 1, values within [0, 1]; its
  values are the device's in full range whatever TV_OUTPUT_ENCODING says
  (the format never stores them encoded: it says how they were loaded),
  so the range of a .cal is its own, not the setting's. Other keywords
  are left out, as the format says; the first table is the calibration.
Anything else is refused with the reason, as before.

The 1D LUT is applied before the 3D table, after the input range, each
channel by its own curve and linearly between the two points around the
signal (the interpolation of a 1D LUT in Resolve, OpenColorIO and a
video card's gamma table), from a texture of its own of 32-bit floats,
its points in rows of 4096, read at the centres of the texels: at its
points it gives the file's values. A 1D LUT alone has the identity of 2
points a side for its 3D table, which the tetrahedral interpolation
gives back to the last places of a float, so every path that takes a
LUT takes it without a case of its own.

Measured (numpy, float64, against the exact curve): a dispcal curve of
256 points applied at its own resolution is within 0.055 of a 10-bit
code of it; baked into a 3D table it would be 0.25 off at 65 points and
1.1 at 17, and a steep curve that opens a crushed black 55 to 99 codes.

The GPU object of a LUT is now struct dvbridge_lut_state (the table's
pl_shader_obj and the texture of the curves), destroyed with
dvbridge_lut_state_destroy(). A LUT without a 1D LUT gives the shader of
before, to the byte. dvbridge_lut_atlas_layout() gives the layout of the
curves' texture for Kodi's own shaders.
